%[text] %[text:anchor:H_26f1] ## Separation of Variables
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0198] ### Method of Separation of Variables
%[text] What makes an equation separable? In a first-order differential equation where $x${"altText":"x"} is the independent variable and $y${"altText":"y"} is the dependent variable, the equation is separable if it can be written in the following form.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(x) \\cdot g(y)\n${"altText":"\frac{dy}{dx} = f(x) \cdot g(y)"}
%[text] Moving all of the $x${"altText":"x"} terms to one side and all of the $y${"altText":"y"} terms to the other, we get:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot dy = f(x) \\cdot dx\n${"altText":"\frac{1}{g(y)} \cdot dy = f(x) \cdot dx"}
%[text] In other words, a separable equation is an equation where all of the $y${"altText":"y"} terms and all of the $x${"altText":"x"} terms can be "separated" on different sides of the equation.
%[text] Once we have the $x${"altText":"x"} and $y${"altText":"y"} terms separated, we can take the integral of both sides of the equation, and then solve for $y${"altText":"y"}.
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:3b49)  You may be asking, why are we able to separate $dy${"altText":"dy"} and $dx${"altText":"dx"}? Isn't this derivative term just a single term?
%[text] Technically that's correct. The derivative term $y' = \\frac{dy}{dx}${"altText":"y' = \frac{dy}{dx}"} is just a single term and not a fraction that we can pull apart. 
%[text] When separating variables, we are actually taking the integral of both sides with respect to $x${"altText":"x"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $g(y) \\cdot y' = f(x)\n${"altText":"g(y) \cdot y' = f(x)"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int{g(y) \\cdot y'} \\; dx = \\int f(x) \\; dx\n${"altText":"\int{g(y) \cdot y'} \; dx = \int f(x) \; dx"}
%[text] Using "u-substitution", we can rewrite the left-hand side, so that the equation looks like the following.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int{g(y)} \\; dy = \\int f(x) \\; dx\n${"altText":"\int{g(y)} \; dy = \int f(x) \; dx"}
%[text] Therefore, even if not technically correct, separating $dy${"altText":"dy"} and $dx${"altText":"dx"} before taking the integral of both sides is convenient shorthand, so feel free to use it when solving using separation of variables.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_52ff] ### Classify Separable Equations
%[text] For each of the following differential equations, select whether or not it is separable. 
%[text] Remember that when $x${"altText":"x"} is the independent variable and $y${"altText":"y"} is the dependent variable, $y'${"altText":"y'"} is equivalent to $\\frac{dy}{dx}${"altText":"\frac{dy}{dx}"}.
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:2176) **Exercise 1.** Consider the following equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 3x^2 \\cdot y${"altText":"\frac{dy}{dx} = 3x^2 \cdot y"} 
CheckExercise1(""); %[control:dropdown:15d5]{"position":[16,18]}
  %[control:button:4db3]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:2449) **Exercise 2.** Consider the following equation. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $xy' = e^x \\cdot y^2${"altText":"xy' = e^x \cdot y^2"}
CheckExercise2(""); %[control:dropdown:75b9]{"position":[16,18]}
  %[control:button:72dc]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:1f6f) **Exercise 3.** Consider the following equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dy}{dx}= 3y + x${"altText":"\frac{dy}{dx}= 3y + x"}
CheckExercise3(""); %[control:dropdown:7f64]{"position":[16,18]}
  %[control:button:7c29]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_414a] ### Example of Separation of Variables
%[text] Consider the following differential equation and initial condition.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ y' = y \\cdot \\cos(t)${"altText":"y' = y \cdot \cos(t)"} $y(0) = 3${"altText":"y(0) = 3"}
%[text] First, remember that we can rewrite $y'${"altText":"y'"} as $\\frac{dy}{dt}${"altText":"\frac{dy}{dt}"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dy}{dt} = y \\cdot \\cos(t)${"altText":"\frac{dy}{dt} = y \cdot \cos(t)"}
%[text] Next, let's separable the variables so that all $y${"altText":"y"} terms are on one side, and all $t${"altText":"t"} terms are on another.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{1}{y} \\; dy = \\cos(t) \\;dt${"altText":"\frac{1}{y} \; dy = \cos(t) \;dt"}
%[text] Next, let's take the integral of both sides of the equation, and solve for $y${"altText":"y"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int \\frac{1}{y} \\; dy = \\int \\cos(t) \\; dt${"altText":"\int \frac{1}{y} \; dy = \int \cos(t) \; dt"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\ln(y) = \\sin(t) + C${"altText":"\ln(y) = \sin(t) + C"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y = e^{\\sin(t) + C}${"altText":"y = e^{\sin(t) + C}"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y = e^{\\sin(t)} \\cdot e^C${"altText":"y = e^{\sin(t)} \cdot e^C"}
%[text] Simplifying $e^C${"altText":"e^C"} to $C${"altText":"C"}, because they are both just arbitrary constants, we get our **general solution.**
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y = Ce^{\\sin(t)}${"altText":"y = Ce^{\sin(t)}"}
%[text] Applying the initial condition $y(0) = 3${"altText":"y(0) = 3"} allows us to find the **particular solution.**
%[text]{"align":"center"} $3 = Ce^{\\sin(0)}${"altText":"3 = Ce^{\sin(0)}"} $3 = Ce^{0}${"altText":"3 = Ce^{0}"} $3 = C${"altText":"3 = C"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y = 3e^{\\sin(t)}${"altText":"y = 3e^{\sin(t)}"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_21cb] ### Practice with Separation of Variables
%[text] Next, let's practice applying this method to solve a new differential equation. 
%[text] ![](text:image:7818)  Some important things to keep in mind when typing math expressions into the edit fields for the exercises:
%[text] - Make sure that you use parentheses carefully.
%[text] - When multiplying values, explicitly use the multiplication symbol. Instead of writing "`2t"`, write `"2*t"`.
%[text] - `"log(x)"` in MATLAB is equivalent to `"ln(x)"` in math class.
%[text] - `"exp(x)"` in MATLAB is equivalent to `"e^x"` in math class. \
%[text] **Exercise 4.**
%[text] Consider the following differential equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{y'}{x} = e^x \\cdot y^2${"altText":"\frac{y'}{x} = e^x \cdot y^2"}
%[text] ![](text:image:8949) a. Find the general solution. Note: use $C${"altText":"C"} for the constant term.
syms x C

Check4a( 0); %[control:editfield:7646]{"position":[9,11]}
  %[control:button:6d92]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
Show4a(false); %[control:checkbox:5348]{"position":[8,13]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:3993) b. Find the particular solution, given the initial condition $y(0) = -1${"altText":"y(0) = -1"}.
syms x

Check4b( 0); %[control:editfield:5ca7]{"position":[9,11]}
  %[control:button:2ef2]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
Show4b(false); %[control:checkbox:1c4f]{"position":[8,13]}
%%
%[text] ## ![](text:image:3ec1) Application: Logistic Population Growth
%[text] An important application of differential equations is in the context of population growth for a species. The logistic growth model assumes that there is a maximum sustainable population of a species, or "carrying capacity", that an environment can handle. 
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![World Population Growth, 1700-2100](text:image:57ab)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [Credit: Max Roser and Hannah Ritchie, CC BY 4.0](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:World_population_growth,_1700-2100,_2022_revision.png)
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] The first-order differential equation for the logistic growth model looks like this.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{dP}{dt} = rP (1 - \\frac{P}{K})${"altText":"\frac{dP}{dt} = rP (1 - \frac{P}{K})"}
%[text] Let's break down each of these variables:
%[text] - $P${"altText":"P"} represents the population of the species, and so it's our **dependent variable**
%[text] - $t${"altText":"t"} represents the elapsed time, and so it's our **independent variable**
%[text] - $K${"altText":"K"}represents the carrying capacity, or maximum population
%[text] - $r${"altText":"r"} represents the growth rate per year- a value of 0.1 means a 10% growth rate per year \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8cd0] ### Human Population
%%
%[text] **Exercise 5.** 
%[text] Let's use this logistic population growth model to try to model human population growth! Our goal is to predict what the human population will be in 2050.
%[text] - In order to simplify our calculations, let's assume that 1 unit of $P${"altText":"P"} represents 1 million people.
%[text] - Additionally, let's assume that 1 unit of $t${"altText":"t"} represents 1 year.
%[text] - The population is expected to peak at approximately 10.5 billion people, so let's assume this number to be our carrying capacity.
%[text] - Let's assume a growth rate of 5% per year. \
%[text] ![](text:image:8be7) a. What is the differential equation that models this scenario?
syms P t
Check5a( 0); %[control:editfield:7eb7]{"position":[9,11]}
  %[control:button:934a]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
Show5a(false); %[control:checkbox:882b]{"position":[8,13]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:92b1) b. Separate the variables. (Hint: make use of partial fraction decomposition to split up the $P${"altText":"P"} term into two fractions that will be easier to integrate.)
syms P t
Check5bDP( 0); %[control:editfield:4da8]{"position":[11,13]}
  %[control:button:355a]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
syms P t
Check5bDT( 0); %[control:editfield:07da]{"position":[11,13]}
  %[control:button:62f1]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
Show5b(false); %[control:checkbox:79a0]{"position":[8,13]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:5f3e) c. Integrate both sides of the equation, and solve for $P${"altText":"P"}. Note: use $C${"altText":"C"} for the constant term.
syms t C
Check5c( 0); %[control:editfield:98b0]{"position":[9,11]}
  %[control:button:07f7]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
Show5c(false); %[control:checkbox:2d24]{"position":[8,13]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:3c0b) d. As of 2024, the human population is approximately 8.2 billion people, and let $t = 0${"altText":"t = 0"} represent 2024. Use this to solve for the constant term $C${"altText":"C"}and find an exact expression for $P${"altText":"P"}. (Hint: Multiply the numerator and denominator of $P${"altText":"P"} by 23 to simplify at the end).
syms t
Check5dC( 0); %[control:editfield:6681]{"position":[10,12]}
  %[control:button:94bc]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
syms t
Check5dP( 0); %[control:editfield:0209]{"position":[10,12]}
  %[control:button:0f02]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
Show5d(false); %[control:checkbox:0409]{"position":[8,13]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:0e92) e. Finally, what does the our solution predict the population will be in 2050? Round to the nearest million people.
syms t
Check5e(0); %[control:editfield:6cd3]{"position":[9,10]}
  %[control:button:1df0]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
Show5e(false); %[control:checkbox:0818]{"position":[8,13]}
%%
%[text] **Exercise 6.** 
%[text] Next, let's generalize the solution for any value of $K${"altText":"K"}, $r${"altText":"r"}, and $P\_0${"altText":"P\_0"}.
%[text] ![](text:image:62c8) a. What is the general solution to the logistic growth model differential equation?
syms K C r t
Check6a( 0); %[control:editfield:1a1a]{"position":[9,11]}
  %[control:button:2052]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
Show6a(false); %[control:checkbox:7522]{"position":[8,13]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:4147) b. What is the particular solution to the logistic growth model differential equation, given $P\_0${"altText":"P\_0"}? Solve for the constant term $C${"altText":"C"}and find an exact expression for $P${"altText":"P"}.
syms K C r t P0
Check6bC( 0); %[control:editfield:8289]{"position":[10,12]}
  %[control:button:2a3d]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
syms K r t P0
Check6bP( 0); %[control:editfield:777a]{"position":[10,12]}
  %[control:button:35a8]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
Show6b(false); %[control:checkbox:7601]{"position":[8,13]}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9bbe] ### **Visualize the Effect of Parameters**
%[text] ![](text:image:7e3f)  Explore how changes to various parameters impact the model!
%[text] Set values for $K${"altText":"K"}, $r${"altText":"r"}, and $P\_0${"altText":"P\_0"}:
kVal = 10500; %[control:editfield:2d16]{"position":[8,13]}
rVal =0.05; %[control:slider:2087]{"position":[7,11]}
P0Val = 8200; %[control:editfield:94a5]{"position":[9,13]}
%[text] Set the range of time values you would like to see in the graph: 
tRange =[-100 50]; %[control:rangeslider:6b32]{"position":[9,18]}
%[text] Specify a specific time where you would like to see the predicted population.
tVal = 26; %[control:editfield:9eb8]{"position":[8,10]}
%[text] Click below to run (or re-run) the graph!
  %[control:button:3ad6]{"position":[1,2]}
ShowLogisticGrowthEquationAndGraph(P0Val, kVal, rVal, tRange, tVal)
